WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.830
Film Courage: Is it possible for writers starting
out today to have the same type of TV writing

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:05.839
career that you had?

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.860
Marc Scott Zicree, Screenwriter/Author: In
terms of writers nowadays having the same

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:15.880
kind of career I had? No, they can't really
because television has changed so much. When

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.519
I started out I started writing in the late
70s and still going strong today. I’ve been

00:00:20.519 --> 00:00:27.960
very lucky. Back then most shows had freelance
slots so in other words for instance when

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.480
I was a producer on Sliders we had a 22 episode
order for that season and 11 of those would

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.390
be freelance slots. So every day we were taking
pitches from writers and we knew we'd have

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:42.060
to buy 11 scripts. Now we might rewrite those
extensively because we knew the show day in

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.060
and day out in a way that the freelancers
wouldn't necessarily but that might be that

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.470
first that person's first sale it might get
them into the industry it gave so many people

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:56.500
chances to get their foot in the door get
a start and and I actually ended up writing

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.700
for so many different shows because sometimes
i'd be on staff but other times i'd just be

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.119
a freelancer coming in and pitching and so
it allowed me also to write for a number of

00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:11.770
different shows much more so than if I was
on staff because you know usually you're exclusive

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:17.159
to that show or you might be able to write
a feature when you're on hiatus but but on

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:23.119
any given year any given day I would be multitasking
I got very very good at multitasking so I

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:27.150
might be well like for instance when I was
a producer on sliders I was writing my own

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.719
scripts helping with every phase of the writing
on everyone else's scripts and but I was also

00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:38.140
I sold to deep space nine that year and far
beyond the stars I had sold a trilogy to harper

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.049
collins so I was starting to write the first
novel with Barbara Hamblin outlining the second

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:47.990
one with Maya Bonhoeff and i'd also been hired
to write all the outlines for Anamorphs and

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:52.819
lay out that show and write several scripts
for that show so friends so I would get into

00:01:52.819 --> 00:01:57.799
my office at universal at six in the morning
write an entire outline for Animorphs and

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:03.810
then by 10 i'd be done with that i'd have
faxed it to New York and then the sliders

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:07.630
guys would come in and i'd do my sliders job
all day and then on the weekend I might be

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:12.870
working on on the novel so we're on the outline
for the deep space nine episodes so it was

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:20.250
it was a great way to work because you know
how to to to go from one thing to another

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.379
to another without losing quality that's that's
the hugely important thing you don't you don't

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:33.019
ever want to be just providing fodder you
don't you don't want to just be writing my

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:37.810
friend Frank Spotnets who create a man in
the high castle and ran the x-files he says

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:42.950
good enough is never good enough and I agree
with that 100 percent he also says you should

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.390
every time you should aim for the bleachers
because you might at least get a single you

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:52.049
know and it's and and I share that view which
is give it your best I mean you should just

00:02:52.049 --> 00:02:57.099
everything you've got should go into everything
you do and and if it's not good enough work

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:02.880
work harder you know but and sometimes you're
not going to write the great American novel

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.520
or the great American screenplay you know
one of my friends who's in Ohio and calls

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.950
me quite often and he said well you know that
episode of whatever show it was forever night

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.560
or whatever the that show that was on last
week wasn't up to your normal standard why

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.290
was that and I said well you know you know
how like when you're in school and sometimes

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:22.280
you really try your best but you get a b on
a test or a c-plus or whatever it's sort of

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:27.230
like that you know you just you know it just
didn't come together and but that but it wasn't

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:33.420
for lack of trying you know and so but that's
part of it and and all but but again but in

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:39.049
terms of possibilities for the writers now
they're much greater for instance when I started

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:45.689
there were three networks and pbs and so they
had and they were 24 7 stations they weren't

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:51.129
you couldn't watch a show when you wanted
to there was no home video and so there were

00:03:51.129 --> 00:03:55.330
only a certain number of shows they could
make just because of the hours and a day so

00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:00.220
it limited how many writers could get work
and it limited the what kind of work you could

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.800
get because if the show wasn't approved by
the network you couldn't there's nothing you

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.670
could do and if you wanted to write something
and shoot it on your own it would cost millions

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:15.209
of dollars minimally and and that was very
very difficult to raise that money now there's

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:22.270
over 500 scripted comedy and drama series
on the air on all the networks and VOD platforms

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:28.750
and and so there's an enormous amount more
slots for writers additionally even though

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.650
there isn't freelance per se there still is
freelance in animation but not in live action

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:38.130
by and large but also people can make their
own web series they can make their own podcasts

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:44.140
there's so many ways to get a following to
get an audience and and and I say everyone

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:48.640
I wouldn't go back to any other period in
my life I'm i'm happiest of all now because

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.840
I have such I have such great autonomy my
audience has given me over two and a half

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:59.630
million dollars to open my own studio and
make space command and and now I'm raising

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:03.479
even more money it turned out I had a knack
and so I don't have to ask permission from

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:08.930
anyone I just come up with an idea I write
it I cast it I shoot it I put it out to my

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:13.699
my audience around the world and that doesn't
mean that the studios and networks and vod

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:18.710
platforms can't be my partners they can but
they aren't the deciding factor of what I

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:26.650
do in terms of what I make I'm in terms of
creatively and and that's a pure joy so going

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:33.210
back to the 70s and then being a freelancer
yeah completely different as to the the tone

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:37.009
of your day what you're working on well it
wasn't so much different that's what I'm working

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:41.849
on because I was always working on shows that
I had a passion for so whether it was sliders

00:05:41.849 --> 00:05:47.819
or even Friday The 13th the series which is
story edited I would always find the core

00:05:47.819 --> 00:05:52.520
of it the emotional core that would resonate
with me it always had to be something that

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:56.550
I could queue into otherwise I wouldn't write
it but and I always worked with people who

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:01.879
I liked and who liked me and I was very lucky
in that when this and the studio executives

00:06:01.879 --> 00:06:08.699
and network executives we dealt with fortunately
very supportive and very smart and so I didn't

00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:13.970
find them intrusive there are the other kind
of executives and and you want to try and

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.250
avoid those in in green lighting yourself
I have a section on working with stupid people

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:24.270
and my first piece of advice is if you can
avoid it avoid it but if you have to work

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:27.449
with someone who's in the food chain they
might be above you in the food chain here's

00:06:27.449 --> 00:06:32.611
how you can do it and not have the work be
ruined and not have and not be fired and so

00:06:32.611 --> 00:06:38.349
I go into some experiences from my career
where I'm able to walk people through that

00:06:38.349 --> 00:06:46.389
and but again it's it wasn't so much content
that was different it was simply it's almost

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:51.520
like there's a difference between performing
a play if you're an actor to an audience versus

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:57.570
auditioning because auditioning you need someone
to approve you in order to do your work creatively

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:02.479
whereas when you're in a play performing for
the audience that's the direct relationship

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:06.849
so the more that I can be in the direct relationship
creatively between the conversations between

00:07:06.849 --> 00:07:12.229
me and the world me and my audience the better
and and really what started me on this journey

00:07:12.229 --> 00:07:16.720
of opening my own studio and doing crowdfunding
and selling investment shares in space command

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.569
and so forth was simply that so many of my
friends who were the greatest writers in television

00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:26.699
had written projects that were great and should
have become hit series and the network or

00:07:26.699 --> 00:07:30.440
the studio cut them off at script or cut them
off at pilot the audience never saw those

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:35.840
great shows and I thought you know in in books
once a novelist is relatively successful then

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.930
he can create a body of work like Stephen
King for instance or name any writer that

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:46.210
you love and and so they have an authorial
voice and a shape to their career that follows

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:50.210
their enthusiasms and what they want to talk
about at any given time but that's not true

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:55.220
with television writers with television writers
for the most part they're constantly stymied

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:59.120
from doing they're the projects they're most
enthusiastic about or the projects that might

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.430
even be their best work because the studio
and the networks have different objectives

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:09.470
which are primarily financial objectives and
I'm and again they have a right to do that

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:13.510
if they're writing the checks but now that
we can go directly to our audience and raise

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:19.319
money that way the fact that we can now shoot
for so much less because digital is so much

00:08:19.319 --> 00:08:27.939
less expensive we can we can change the rules
to into our favor and you know it used to

00:08:27.939 --> 00:08:32.370
be when when young people would like young
directors or writers would say well I'm they're

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.280
not letting me get have my dream and I would
say well who's not letting you have your dream

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.669
they say well the networks are the studios
or whatever and back when it cost millions

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:43.539
of dollars to make a movie you'd say okay
well then that makes sense they are being

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:48.040
stymied but now since you know they have a
camera in their pocket I don't I don't accept

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:54.930
that excuse anymore someone says they're not
letting me have my dream i'll say you are

00:08:54.930 --> 00:09:00.990
you're using an excuse to avoid doing the
work because the work is get a camera get

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:05.079
some people together get the best best actors
you can find make it happen one way or another

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:11.130
make it happen and a great minute is better
than a mediocre two hours of material but

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:16.190
if people love it you'll start to build your
audience that starts to build a career how

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:21.690
did fear of success almost ruin your screenwriting
career and when you hear that from these these

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:28.130
other writers yes do you hear fear of success
in their voice or it's it's asking for permission

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:32.740
I think there's always a counterweight between
fear of success and fear of failure in everyone's

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:38.290
heart in one way or another in different measures
one is always afraid of exposure one is always

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:47.180
afraid of screwing it up you know and and
ultimately you have to be willing to face

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:51.740
that and get past it and get over it and get
around it you your job is to get your work

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:57.170
done your creative work and with all of my
mentors whether it's rod sterling or Ray Bradbury

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:03.260
or Guillermo Del Toro with whom I wrote a
book not long ago I seek out mentors who are

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:08.329
brilliant at what they do and then one of
my key questions always is when you encounter

00:10:08.329 --> 00:10:14.720
failure or when you encounter rejection what
do you do and because what I'm looking for

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.360
are strategies that I can apply to my own
life in my own career that keep me moving

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:26.450
forward and and it's been incredibly invaluable
because you you find that the higher up someone

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:33.180
goes as a writer or a director or whatever
or an actor their fear is still there but

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:38.910
they have coping mechanisms where the period
that they're in breakdown is shorter and shorter

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:43.910
and shorter so in other words for instance
when Guillermo Del Toro was preparing to film

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:49.220
his dream project the mountains of madness
based on an hp Lovecraft novel they pulled

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:52.990
the plug on it universal did because they
thought it would be too expensive and r-rated

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:56.910
and so forth and and Guillermo had other other
irons in the fire and immediately jumped over

00:10:56.910 --> 00:11:00.709
to pacific rim and he was off and running
so that didn't mean he didn't feel terrible

00:11:00.709 --> 00:11:05.029
and maybe had a weekend of feeling very sorry
for himself but he got into motion and he

00:11:05.029 --> 00:11:10.600
was already preparing for that eventuality
eventuality in advance so that he wouldn't

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:18.600
be stopped and it's very important you know
I think don't be afraid of success because

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:24.149
success is very nice you know when you write
a book or that's successful or a tv show that

00:11:24.149 --> 00:11:32.380
people talk about for decades or you get a
big payday those things are fine they're fine

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:37.660
I think people are often afraid of scrutiny
they're often afraid of well I'm a fraud and

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:43.360
I won't be able to handle success but success
is an extremely amorphous thing you first

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:47.200
of all you can define what success means to
you for instance at this point in my life

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:51.720
I don't need to have the million dollar payday
you know I need to keep working creatively

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:55.329
and getting my work out to an audience but
money is not at all what rules me I've made

00:11:55.329 --> 00:11:59.839
choices we still live in an apartment we own
our two cars outright you know it's I've made

00:11:59.839 --> 00:12:06.820
choices that allow my life to be very free
in that way but but for me success is very

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:13.140
very clear and I'm glad that I've been successful
you know in I mean I you know I can look at

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:16.891
my career and say well clearly I've succeeded
because I've created work that people are

00:12:16.891 --> 00:12:22.199
still talking about you know 40 years after
I did it and also I've earned significant

00:12:22.199 --> 00:12:27.560
money writing for the studios and the networks
until space command i'd only written for the

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:32.260
studios and the networks and the major publishers
i'd never done any independent film projects

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:36.160
and then you know because of this roundtable
I mentor I started hearing about crowdfunding

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:42.420
and I thought well let's see if I can raise
money and turned out I could so even though

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:48.661
rod sterling was considered a dashingly handsome
confident man angry at times maybe do you

00:12:48.661 --> 00:12:54.450
think he had a touch of fear of success did
he mask it with or sort of the the this pretty

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:59.340
boy you know I don't think he was afraid of
success he was extremely charismatic extremely

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:04.740
confident without being arrogant but he felt
that he had failed in his career which is

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:10.990
terribly sad the last interview he gave before
he died at age 50 in in 1975 the interviewer

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:15.410
said do you think you've been successful you
do think you've you've written work that will

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:20.730
stand the test of time and he said the good
writing like fine wine has to age well with

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:26.820
time he said I think my work has been momentarily
adequate which is astonishing and so sad that

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:31.029
he didn't live until now because he would
have seen that he had created one of the greatest

00:13:31.029 --> 00:13:36.530
masterpieces of television and he had inspired
an entire generation of showrunners I mean

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:41.380
every major showrunner in drama rod is their
hero he's the idol he's the guy who sort of

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:48.410
led the way as and it's really really sad
that he didn't get to to know that you know

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:55.220
I'm sure he would have been amazed and extremely
gratified sure many other Philip K Dick same

00:13:55.220 --> 00:14:00.000
thing fell okay dick was a different case
because he was at least he got to see fellow

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:06.279
Dick at least got to see the footage from
blade runner he knew that somebody was getting

00:14:06.279 --> 00:14:11.829
getting what he was doing and so he and he
my god he created a huge amount of work I

00:14:11.829 --> 00:14:16.880
mean he wrote I think about 12 novels in a
year plus short stories I mean it's like let's

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:23.270
hear from amphetamines you know so yes that
I think that did help fuel him but also he

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:28.579
had his own what did he call the hovel which
was his sort of grungy little yeah he was

00:14:28.579 --> 00:14:31.570
he was a talented man he was very funny he
was a troubled man and there was all this

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:36.339
whole thing of how drugs drove him crazy and
at the end he was raving and and insane and

00:14:36.339 --> 00:14:41.540
all that stuff and and I had dinner not long
ago with Greg Benford who's a fairly conservative

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:45.810
professor and also a science fiction writer
and I taught we've got on the subject Philip

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:50.060
K. Dick and he said oh no no Phil wasn't like
that at all he was very sweet very funny his

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:54.730
mind was perfectly together and he knew him
you know throughout the latter years of his

00:14:54.730 --> 00:15:00.440
life and so I was very glad to hear that because
I think sometimes the media can get an impression

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:05.331
of someone which isn't accurate and Philip
K Dick was an amazing writer and I'm glad

00:15:05.331 --> 00:15:06.331
that that he had friends who really saw who
he was and valued that.

00:15:06.331 --> 00:15:07.331
Question For The Viewers:

00:15:07.331 --> 00:15:07.341
When you encounter failure or rejection what
do you do?

